---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Sources from '../components/Sources.astro';
import YouTube from '../components/YouTube.astro';
import { SRC, VIDEOS, LAST_UPDATED } from '../data/site';
import { articleSchema } from '../lib/schema';

const path = '/world/';
const title = 'Astrae Oratio World & Lore Explained: Tokyo ’89, Magic, Kleis, Artifacts and Dominions';
const description =
  'The world of Astrae Oratio explained: Tokyo ’89 (1889, a century ahead of history), what magic is, Hecate, the Kleis and Artifacts, dominions, Trials of Domination, the Eras of magic and the Special District Office.';
const schema = [articleSchema({ headline: title, description, path, published: new Date('2026-10-07'), modified: new Date(LAST_UPDATED) })];

const toc = [
  ['synopsis', 'Synopsis'],
  ['tokyo-89', 'Tokyo ’89: 1889 or 1989?'],
  ['magic', 'What magic is'],
  ['magicians', 'Magicians'],
  ['hecate', 'Hecate, the first magician'],
  ['kleis', 'Kleis (ignition key)'],
  ['artifact', 'Artifacts'],
  ['dominion', 'Dominions'],
  ['trial', 'Trial of Domination'],
  ['eras', 'The Eras of magic'],
  ['tower', 'The Tower'],
  ['sdo', 'The Special District Office'],
];
---

<Base
  {title}
  {description}
  {path}
  {schema}
  ogType="article"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { name: 'World & lore', href: path },
  ]}
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="kicker">World · Lore</p>
      <h1>The World of Astrae Oratio</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Retro, magic, administration, adventure, Shindenki: the official site’s own tagline sums up the mix. This guide walks
        through the setting and the key lore concepts, in plain language, with links to the characters and systems they touch.
      </p>
      <p class="meta-line">Last updated <time datetime={LAST_UPDATED}>{LAST_UPDATED}</time></p>
    </header>

    <div class="with-aside">
      <article class="prose">
        <h2 id="synopsis">Synopsis</h2>
        <p>
          In a Tokyo that feels familiar but isn’t quite ours, Tokyo Tower has only just been finished and the international
          exhibition center in Odaiba is still a building site. The <a href="/characters/shunin/">Shunin</a>, a low-ranking local
          civil servant, is abruptly reassigned to an agency nobody has heard of: the
          <a href="/factions/#special-district-office">Special District Office</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The office turns out to run the administrative affairs of every magician living secretly in Tokyo — and the Shunin is
          now the one official responsible for all of them. Any incident involving the city’s magicians is his to handle, alone.
        </p>

        <h2 id="tokyo-89">Tokyo ’89: 1889 or 1989?</h2>
        <p>
          The setting is marketed as <strong>Tokyo ’89</strong>, which causes some confusion. The official answer is
          <strong>1889</strong> — but in a world where technology, culture and civilization have raced roughly a
          <strong>hundred years ahead</strong> of real history. The result looks and feels like late-1980s Japan at the height of
          its postwar boom — some Japanese coverage simply calls the look “Heisei retro” and lists the year as 1989.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Tokyo beat its rival <strong>Kyoto</strong> to host the World Expo after Paris, so Odaiba is being reclaimed at full speed.</li>
          <li><strong>Tokyo Tower</strong> is due to be completed this year.</li>
          <li>The city is seen as the most youthful and energetic in the world.</li>
          <li>In-universe, the year is <strong>Reisei 9</strong> (令成9年), using the game’s fictional era name.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The real 1889 is a deliberate choice: it is the year of the Paris Exposition and the Eiffel Tower, and the year
          Nietzsche collapsed in Turin — both of which the lore folds into its history of magic (see <a href="#tower">The Tower</a>).
        </p>

        <h2 id="magic">What magic is</h2>
        <p>
          In Astrae Oratio, <strong>magic is human dreams</strong>. Throughout history, people who wanted something from the world —
          a wish, a prayer, a hope, even a fear — looked up and spoke it to the stars, knowing it might never come true. Those
          dreams, the lore says, became the stars themselves. The game’s title echoes the idea: <em>astra</em> (stars) and
          <em>oratio</em> (speech, prayer).
        </p>

        <h2 id="magicians">Magicians</h2>
        <p>
          A <strong>magician</strong> is someone who takes the wishes people placed in the stars and makes them real with their own
          hands. The defining trait of modern magic is that it <strong>reflects the way of life of the land the magician lives
          in</strong> — which is why a Minato middle schooler, a Shinjuku urban-legend hunter and a Bunkyo vigilante all cast very
          differently. Magicians generally hide what they are from ordinary people.
        </p>

        <h2 id="hecate">Hecate, the first magician</h2>
        <p>
          The goddess <strong>Hecate</strong> is revered as the first magician. A goddess of sorcery, the moon and the night,
          crossroads and boundaries, she governs every passage between worlds: day and night, life and death, the surface and the
          hidden side, city and nature, reality and unreality.
        </p>
        <p>
          She is usually depicted with a <strong>torch</strong> and a <strong>dagger</strong>. The torch marks the line between
          reality and unreality — the key into magic. The dagger is the magician’s will, which makes magic manifest. Myths have
          faded since, but those two ideas survive as the Kleis and the Artifact.
        </p>

        <h2 id="kleis">Kleis (ignition key)</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Kleis</strong> (κλείς) is ancient Greek for “key”. It is Hecate’s torch in modern form: the basic tool every
          magician needs to cross into the hidden side of reality, where magic works. Magicians call it the
          <strong>ignition key</strong> — like starting a car, you turn it to switch into magic. Since every magician owns one, a
          Kleis also serves as proof that someone is a magician.
        </p>

        <h2 id="artifact">Artifacts</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Artifacts</strong> are Hecate’s dagger: magic weapons through which a magician’s will takes form. They come in
          every shape, from antiques with centuries of history to modern inventions. Once a magician has entered the hidden side
          with their Kleis, an Artifact lets them use far stronger magic than usual. The developers have also named
          <a href="/guides/combat/#artifact-liberation">Artifact Liberation</a> as a combat mechanic.
        </p>

        <h2 id="dominion">Dominions</h2>
        <p>
          Because magic mirrors the life of the land, a magician’s territory — their <strong>dominion</strong> — is the foundation of
          their power. Since the Fifth Era, magicians have borrowed <strong>administration</strong> as their framework for
          understanding the world, systematizing the administrative structure of the places they belong to.
        </p>
        <p>
          In modern Tokyo that means the <strong>23 special wards and other districts double as magician dominions</strong>: Minato,
          Shinjuku, Shibuya, Shinagawa, Sumida and so on. Conflicts between magicians therefore play out as
          <strong>administrative disputes</strong> — and mediating them is the Shunin’s job. In battle, this idea becomes the
          <a href="/guides/combat/#dominion-skill">Dominion Skill</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="trial">Trial of Domination</h2>
        <p>
          When magicians clash, they settle it with a <strong>Trial of Domination</strong> — a trial by duel. It has been the main way
          of resolving disputes, and effectively the magicians’ justice system, since the Fourth Era.
        </p>

        <h2 id="eras">The Eras of magic</h2>
        <p>
          Magic history is divided into numbered <strong>Eras</strong>. Two endings are known: the <strong>Second Era</strong>
          ended when the magician-king of Babylon, who built a tower to become a god, fell back to earth; the
          <strong>Fifth Era</strong> ends in <strong>Reisei 9</strong> — the year the story starts — echoing the philosopher in Turin
          who declared that God is dead and lost his mind. The game begins at the hinge between two eras.
        </p>

        <h2 id="tower">The Tower</h2>
        <p>
          Between the Tower of Babel and the Eiffel Tower — between “I will become a god” and “God is dead” — the
          <strong>tower</strong> keeps returning as a symbol of humanity reaching for the stars instead of merely wishing on them.
          As the Fifth Era closes, a new tower is nearing completion in Tokyo, as if announcing the next paradigm of magic. That
          tower is Tokyo Tower, and the Special District Office sits right beneath it.
        </p>

        <h2 id="sdo">The Special District Office</h2>
        <p>
          Officially the <strong>Other Decree Special District Office</strong> (裏令指定特例区域管理庁), the
          <a href="/factions/#special-district-office">Special District Office</a> administers the special district designated by
          the <a href="/factions/#other-cabinet">Other Cabinet</a> — the hidden power that really runs magical Tokyo. That gives it
          authority over every magical matter in the city. People call it the magicians’ Tokyo Metropolitan Government, but at
          the moment its staff is exactly one person: the Shunin.
        </p>

        <YouTube id={VIDEOS.prologueAnimation.id} title={VIDEOS.prologueAnimation.title} />

        <p>
          Continue with the <a href="/factions/">factions guide</a>, the <a href="/glossary/">glossary</a> or the
          <a href="/characters/">character list</a>.
        </p>

        <Sources items={[SRC.officialWorld, SRC.invenBreakdown, SRC.gematsuRelease]} updated={LAST_UPDATED} />
      </article>

      <aside>
        <nav class="card toc" aria-label="On this page">
          <h2>On this page</h2>
          <ol>
            {
              toc.map(([id, label]) => (
                <li>
                  <a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ol>
        </nav>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
